{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/sarif-2.1/schema/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "fullName": "Docker Scout",
          "informationUri": "https://docker.com/products/docker-scout",
          "name": "docker scout",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "GHSA-xg2h-wx96-xgxr",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GHSA-xg2h-wx96-xgxr: OWASP Top Ten 2017 Category A9 - Using Components with Known Vulnerabilities"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/GHSA-xg2h-wx96-xgxr?s=gitlab&n=goutils&ns=github.com%2Fmasterminds&t=golang&vr=%3C1.1.1",
              "help": {
                "text": "A security-sensitive bug was discovered by Open Source Developer *Erik Sundell of Sundell Open Source Consulting AB*.\n\nThe functions `RandomAlphaNumeric(int)` and `CryptoRandomAlphaNumeric(int)` are not as random as they should be. Small values of `int` in the functions above will return a smaller subset of results than they should. For example, `RandomAlphaNumeric(1)` will always return a digit in the 0-9 range, while `RandomAlphaNumeric(4)` will return around ~7 million of the ~13M possible permutations.\n\nThis is considered a security release because programs that rely upon random generators for passwords are at an increased risk of brute force-style password guessing. There is also a higher probability of collision.\n\nThe problem was the result of a mistaken regular expression that only accepted random strings if they contained a digit from `[0-9]`. That restriction has been removed.\n",
                "markdown": "> A security-sensitive bug was discovered by Open Source Developer *Erik Sundell of Sundell Open Source Consulting AB*.\n\nThe functions `RandomAlphaNumeric(int)` and `CryptoRandomAlphaNumeric(int)` are not as random as they should be. Small values of `int` in the functions above will return a smaller subset of results than they should. For example, `RandomAlphaNumeric(1)` will always return a digit in the 0-9 range, while `RandomAlphaNumeric(4)` will return around ~7 million of the ~13M possible permutations.\n\nThis is considered a security release because programs that rely upon random generators for passwords are at an increased risk of brute force-style password guessing. There is also a higher probability of collision.\n\nThe problem was the result of a mistaken regular expression that only accepted random strings if they contained a digit from `[0-9]`. That restriction has been removed.\n\n|                                                                 |\n|Package         pkg:golang/github.com/masterminds/goutils@1.1.0  |\n|Affected range  <1.1.1                                           |\n|Fixed version   1.1.1                                            |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.1.1",
                "cvssV3_severity": "UNSPECIFIED",
                "fixed_version": "1.1.1",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/github.com/masterminds/goutils@1.1.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "UNSPECIFIED"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2021-4238",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2021-4238: Insufficient Entropy"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2021-4238?s=github&n=goutils&ns=github.com%2Fmasterminds&t=golang&vr=%3C1.1.1",
              "help": {
                "text": "### Impact\n\nA security-sensitive bug was discovered by Open Source Developer *Erik Sundell of Sundell Open Source Consulting AB*.\n\nThe functions `RandomAlphaNumeric(int)` and `CryptoRandomAlphaNumeric(int)` are not as random as they should be. Small values of `int` in the functions above will return a smaller subset of results than they should. For example, `RandomAlphaNumeric(1)` will always return a digit in the 0-9 range, while `RandomAlphaNumeric(4)` will return around ~7 million of the ~13M possible permutations.\n\nThis is considered a security release because programs that rely upon random generators for passwords are at an increased risk of brute force-style password guessing. There is also a higher probability of collision.\n\nThe problem was the result of a mistaken regular expression that only accepted random strings if they contained a digit from `[0-9]`. That restriction has been removed.\n\n### Patches\n\nThis issue has been corrected in v1.1.1.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nIf you cannot upgrade to v1.1.1, you can work around the issue by calling `RandomAlphaNumericCustom(N, true, true)`|`CryptoRandomAlphaNumericCustom(N, true, true)`  instead. (Where `N` is the desired length, and `true` is the literal boolean `true`.)\n",
                "markdown": "> ### Impact\n\nA security-sensitive bug was discovered by Open Source Developer *Erik Sundell of Sundell Open Source Consulting AB*.\n\nThe functions `RandomAlphaNumeric(int)` and `CryptoRandomAlphaNumeric(int)` are not as random as they should be. Small values of `int` in the functions above will return a smaller subset of results than they should. For example, `RandomAlphaNumeric(1)` will always return a digit in the 0-9 range, while `RandomAlphaNumeric(4)` will return around ~7 million of the ~13M possible permutations.\n\nThis is considered a security release because programs that rely upon random generators for passwords are at an increased risk of brute force-style password guessing. There is also a higher probability of collision.\n\nThe problem was the result of a mistaken regular expression that only accepted random strings if they contained a digit from `[0-9]`. That restriction has been removed.\n\n### Patches\n\nThis issue has been corrected in v1.1.1.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nIf you cannot upgrade to v1.1.1, you can work around the issue by calling `RandomAlphaNumericCustom(N, true, true)`|`CryptoRandomAlphaNumericCustom(N, true, true)`  instead. (Where `N` is the desired length, and `true` is the literal boolean `true`.)\n\n|                                                                 |\n|Package         pkg:golang/github.com/masterminds/goutils@1.1.0  |\n|Affected range  <1.1.1                                           |\n|Fixed version   1.1.1                                            |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.1.1",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "1.1.1",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/github.com/masterminds/goutils@1.1.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-6237",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-6237"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-6237?s=ubuntu&n=openssl&ns=ubuntu&t=deb&osn=ubuntu&osv=22.04&vr=%3C3.0.2-0ubuntu1.14",
              "help": {
                "text": "Issue summary: Checking excessively long invalid RSA public keys may take a long time.  Impact summary: Applications that use the function EVP_PKEY_public_check() to check RSA public keys may experience long delays. Where the key that is being checked has been obtained from an untrusted source this may lead to a Denial of Service.  When function EVP_PKEY_public_check() is called on RSA public keys, a computation is done to confirm that the RSA modulus, n, is composite. For valid RSA keys, n is a product of two or more large primes and this computation completes quickly. However, if n is an overly large prime, then this computation would take a long time.  An application that calls EVP_PKEY_public_check() and supplies an RSA key obtained from an untrusted source could be vulnerable to a Denial of Service attack.  The function EVP_PKEY_public_check() is not called from other OpenSSL functions however it is called from the OpenSSL pkey command line application. For that reason that application is also vulnerable if used with the '-pubin' and '-check' options on untrusted data.  The OpenSSL SSL/TLS implementation is not affected by this issue.  The OpenSSL 3.0 and 3.1 FIPS providers are affected by this issue.\n",
                "markdown": "> Issue summary: Checking excessively long invalid RSA public keys may take a long time.  Impact summary: Applications that use the function EVP_PKEY_public_check() to check RSA public keys may experience long delays. Where the key that is being checked has been obtained from an untrusted source this may lead to a Denial of Service.  When function EVP_PKEY_public_check() is called on RSA public keys, a computation is done to confirm that the RSA modulus, n, is composite. For valid RSA keys, n is a product of two or more large primes and this computation completes quickly. However, if n is an overly large prime, then this computation would take a long time.  An application that calls EVP_PKEY_public_check() and supplies an RSA key obtained from an untrusted source could be vulnerable to a Denial of Service attack.  The function EVP_PKEY_public_check() is not called from other OpenSSL functions however it is called from the OpenSSL pkey command line application. For that reason that application is also vulnerable if used with the '-pubin' and '-check' options on untrusted data.  The OpenSSL SSL/TLS implementation is not affected by this issue.  The OpenSSL 3.0 and 3.1 FIPS providers are affected by this issue.\n\n|                                                                                                         |\n|Package         pkg:deb/ubuntu/openssl@3.0.2-0ubuntu1.6?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04  |\n|Affected range  <3.0.2-0ubuntu1.14                                                                       |\n|Fixed version   3.0.2-0ubuntu1.14                                                                        |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<3.0.2-0ubuntu1.14",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "3.0.2-0ubuntu1.14",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/ubuntu/openssl@3.0.2-0ubuntu1.6?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-11053",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2024-11053"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2024-11053?s=ubuntu&n=curl&ns=ubuntu&t=deb&osn=ubuntu&osv=22.04&vr=%3C7.81.0-1ubuntu1.20",
              "help": {
                "text": "When asked to both use a `.netrc` file for credentials and to follow HTTP redirects, curl could leak the password used for the first host to the followed-to host under certain circumstances.  This flaw only manifests itself if the netrc file has an entry that matches the redirect target hostname but the entry either omits just the password or omits both login and password.\n",
                "markdown": "> When asked to both use a `.netrc` file for credentials and to follow HTTP redirects, curl could leak the password used for the first host to the followed-to host under certain circumstances.  This flaw only manifests itself if the netrc file has an entry that matches the redirect target hostname but the entry either omits just the password or omits both login and password.\n\n|                                                                                                       |\n|Package         pkg:deb/ubuntu/curl@7.81.0-1ubuntu1.3?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04  |\n|Affected range  <7.81.0-1ubuntu1.20                                                                    |\n|Fixed version   7.81.0-1ubuntu1.20                                                                     |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<7.81.0-1ubuntu1.20",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "7.81.0-1ubuntu1.20",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/ubuntu/curl@7.81.0-1ubuntu1.3?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-13176",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2024-13176"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2024-13176?s=ubuntu&n=openssl&ns=ubuntu&t=deb&osn=ubuntu&osv=22.04&vr=%3C3.0.2-0ubuntu1.19",
              "help": {
                "text": "Issue summary: A timing side-channel which could potentially allow recovering the private key exists in the ECDSA signature computation.  Impact summary: A timing side-channel in ECDSA signature computations could allow recovering the private key by an attacker. However, measuring the timing would require either local access to the signing application or a very fast network connection with low latency.  There is a timing signal of around 300 nanoseconds when the top word of the inverted ECDSA nonce value is zero. This can happen with significant probability only for some of the supported elliptic curves. In particular the NIST P-521 curve is affected. To be able to measure this leak, the attacker process must either be located in the same physical computer or must have a very fast network connection with low latency. For that reason the severity of this vulnerability is Low.  The FIPS modules in 3.4, 3.3, 3.2, 3.1 and 3.0 are affected by this issue.\n",
                "markdown": "> Issue summary: A timing side-channel which could potentially allow recovering the private key exists in the ECDSA signature computation.  Impact summary: A timing side-channel in ECDSA signature computations could allow recovering the private key by an attacker. However, measuring the timing would require either local access to the signing application or a very fast network connection with low latency.  There is a timing signal of around 300 nanoseconds when the top word of the inverted ECDSA nonce value is zero. This can happen with significant probability only for some of the supported elliptic curves. In particular the NIST P-521 curve is affected. To be able to measure this leak, the attacker process must either be located in the same physical computer or must have a very fast network connection with low latency. For that reason the severity of this vulnerability is Low.  The FIPS modules in 3.4, 3.3, 3.2, 3.1 and 3.0 are affected by this issue.\n\n|                                                                                                         |\n|Package         pkg:deb/ubuntu/openssl@3.0.2-0ubuntu1.6?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04  |\n|Affected range  <3.0.2-0ubuntu1.19                                                                       |\n|Fixed version   3.0.2-0ubuntu1.19                                                                        |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<3.0.2-0ubuntu1.19",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "3.0.2-0ubuntu1.19",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/ubuntu/openssl@3.0.2-0ubuntu1.6?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-2236",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2024-2236"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2024-2236?s=ubuntu&n=libgcrypt20&ns=ubuntu&t=deb&osn=ubuntu&osv=22.04&vr=%3E%3D0",
              "help": {
                "text": "A timing-based side-channel flaw was found in libgcrypt's RSA implementation. This issue may allow a remote attacker to initiate a Bleichenbacher-style attack, which can lead to the decryption of RSA ciphertexts.\n",
                "markdown": "> A timing-based side-channel flaw was found in libgcrypt's RSA implementation. This issue may allow a remote attacker to initiate a Bleichenbacher-style attack, which can lead to the decryption of RSA ciphertexts.\n\n|                                                                                                           |\n|Package         pkg:deb/ubuntu/libgcrypt20@1.9.4-3ubuntu3?os_distro=jammy&os_name=ubuntu&os_version=22.04  |\n|Affected range  >=0                                                                                        |\n|Fixed version   not fixed                                                                                  |\n"
