            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-39325",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-39325"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-39325?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3C1.20.10",
              "help": {
                "text": "A malicious HTTP/2 client which rapidly creates requests and immediately resets them can cause excessive server resource consumption. While the total number of requests is bounded by the http2.Server.MaxConcurrentStreams setting, resetting an in-progress request allows the attacker to create a new request while the existing one is still executing.\n\nWith the fix applied, HTTP/2 servers now bound the number of simultaneously executing handler goroutines to the stream concurrency limit (MaxConcurrentStreams). New requests arriving when at the limit (which can only happen after the client has reset an existing, in-flight request) will be queued until a handler exits. If the request queue grows too large, the server will terminate the connection.\n\nThis issue is also fixed in golang.org/x/net/http2 for users manually configuring HTTP/2.\n\nThe default stream concurrency limit is 250 streams (requests) per HTTP/2 connection. This value may be adjusted using the golang.org/x/net/http2 package; see the Server.MaxConcurrentStreams setting and the ConfigureServer function.\n",
                "markdown": "> A malicious HTTP/2 client which rapidly creates requests and immediately resets them can cause excessive server resource consumption. While the total number of requests is bounded by the http2.Server.MaxConcurrentStreams setting, resetting an in-progress request allows the attacker to create a new request while the existing one is still executing.\n\nWith the fix applied, HTTP/2 servers now bound the number of simultaneously executing handler goroutines to the stream concurrency limit (MaxConcurrentStreams). New requests arriving when at the limit (which can only happen after the client has reset an existing, in-flight request) will be queued until a handler exits. If the request queue grows too large, the server will terminate the connection.\n\nThis issue is also fixed in golang.org/x/net/http2 for users manually configuring HTTP/2.\n\nThe default stream concurrency limit is 250 streams (requests) per HTTP/2 connection. This value may be adjusted using the golang.org/x/net/http2 package; see the Server.MaxConcurrentStreams setting and the ConfigureServer function.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.17.5  |\n|Affected range  <1.20.10                  |\n|Fixed version   1.20.10                   |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.20.10",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "1.20.10",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.0.0-20201110031124-69a78807bb2b",
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.0.0-20220107192237-5cfca573fb4d",
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.0.0-20220621193019-9d032be2e588",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.16.5",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.17",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.17.5",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.18.4"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-45142",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-45142"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-45142?s=golang&n=client_golang&ns=github.com%2Fprometheus&t=golang&vr=%3C1.11.1",
              "help": {
                "text": "The Prometheus client_golang HTTP server is vulnerable to a denial of service attack when handling requests with non-standard HTTP methods.\n\nIn order to be affected, an instrumented software must use any of the promhttp.InstrumentHandler* middleware except RequestsInFlight; not filter any specific methods (e.g GET) before middleware; pass a metric with a \"method\" label name to a middleware; and not have any firewall/LB/proxy that filters away requests with unknown \"method\".\n",
                "markdown": "> The Prometheus client_golang HTTP server is vulnerable to a denial of service attack when handling requests with non-standard HTTP methods.\n\nIn order to be affected, an instrumented software must use any of the promhttp.InstrumentHandler* middleware except RequestsInFlight; not filter any specific methods (e.g GET) before middleware; pass a metric with a \"method\" label name to a middleware; and not have any firewall/LB/proxy that filters away requests with unknown \"method\".\n\n|                                                                       |\n|Package         pkg:golang/github.com/prometheus/client_golang@1.11.0  |\n|Affected range  <1.11.1                                                |\n|Fixed version   1.11.1                                                 |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.11.1",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "1.11.1",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/github.com/prometheus/client_golang@1.11.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-45283",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-45283"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-45283?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3C1.20.11",
              "help": {
                "text": "The filepath package does not recognize paths with a \\??\\ prefix as special.\n\nOn Windows, a path beginning with \\??\\ is a Root Local Device path equivalent to a path beginning with \\\\?\\. Paths with a \\??\\ prefix may be used to access arbitrary locations on the system. For example, the path \\??\\c:\\x is equivalent to the more common path c:\\x.\n\nBefore fix, Clean could convert a rooted path such as \\a\\..\\??\\b into the root local device path \\??\\b. Clean will now convert this to .\\??\\b.\n\nSimilarly, Join(\\, ??, b) could convert a seemingly innocent sequence of path elements into the root local device path \\??\\b. Join will now convert this to \\.\\??\\b.\n\nIn addition, with fix, IsAbs now correctly reports paths beginning with \\??\\ as absolute, and VolumeName correctly reports the \\??\\ prefix as a volume name.\n\nUPDATE: Go 1.20.11 and Go 1.21.4 inadvertently changed the definition of the volume name in Windows paths starting with \\?, resulting in filepath.Clean(\\?\\c:) returning \\?\\c: rather than \\?\\c:\\ (among other effects). The previous behavior has been restored.\n",
                "markdown": "> The filepath package does not recognize paths with a \\??\\ prefix as special.\n\nOn Windows, a path beginning with \\??\\ is a Root Local Device path equivalent to a path beginning with \\\\?\\. Paths with a \\??\\ prefix may be used to access arbitrary locations on the system. For example, the path \\??\\c:\\x is equivalent to the more common path c:\\x.\n\nBefore fix, Clean could convert a rooted path such as \\a\\..\\??\\b into the root local device path \\??\\b. Clean will now convert this to .\\??\\b.\n\nSimilarly, Join(\\, ??, b) could convert a seemingly innocent sequence of path elements into the root local device path \\??\\b. Join will now convert this to \\.\\??\\b.\n\nIn addition, with fix, IsAbs now correctly reports paths beginning with \\??\\ as absolute, and VolumeName correctly reports the \\??\\ prefix as a volume name.\n\nUPDATE: Go 1.20.11 and Go 1.21.4 inadvertently changed the definition of the volume name in Windows paths starting with \\?, resulting in filepath.Clean(\\?\\c:) returning \\?\\c: rather than \\?\\c:\\ (among other effects). The previous behavior has been restored.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.18.4  |\n|Affected range  <1.20.11                  |\n|Fixed version   1.20.11                   |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.20.11",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "1.20.11",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.16.5",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.17",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.17.5",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.18.4"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
